body:
A declarative agent for maintenance technicians and planners in a plant or MRO organisation. It reads released manuals and safety checklists from two SharePoint locations, work order history from a Copilot connector that indexes the CMMS, and downtime data through the Fabric capability. It is deliberately a lookup and analysis helper: it never states that equipment is serviceable or released. Replace the placeholder URLs, the connector ID and the machine wording with your own. All names are examples.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Microsoft 365 Copilot licensing for every user of the agent. The reference page says users can open an agent only if they meet the requirements of every capability it uses, so check each capability you keep.
- Schema version: v1.9. The Microsoft Learn manifest reference (opened 20 November 2026) documents v1.9 and the what's-new page lists 1.9 as the latest, released October 2026. The reference page marks instructions as required while the what's-new page says it became optional in 1.9; this manifest always sets it. Unrecognised properties make the whole manifest invalid, so remove comments before you deploy.
- Limits used here: strings up to 4,000 characters unless stated, name up to 100, description up to 1,000, instructions up to 8,000, disclaimer text up to 500, at most 12 conversation starters, one object per capability type.
- A SharePoint library that holds only released revisions, a Copilot connector connection for the CMMS (see the connectors entry in this library) and, for the Fabric capability, the Fabric requirements described on Microsoft Learn; check in your tenant what the capability needs and remove it if you cannot meet them.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: capability order does not matter. depends_on makes the downtime starter appear only when the Fabric capability is present.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Plant Maintenance Assistant",
 "description": "Answers maintenance questions from released manuals and safety checklists, looks up work order history from a Copilot connector and analyses downtime data in Fabric.",
 "instructions": "You are the Plant Maintenance Assistant for maintenance technicians and planners.\n\nScope\n- Questions about machine manuals, lubrication and inspection routines, safety checklists and past work orders.\n- Downtime and failure analysis when Fabric data is available.\n\nRules\n1. Answer only from the configured sources. Name the document, its revision and the section or page you used. If the revision is missing, say so.\n2. If the sources do not contain the answer, say that and name who to ask (the maintenance planner). Do not guess.\n3. Work order history comes from the CMMS connector. Quote the work order number for every statement about past jobs.\n4. For safety questions give the checklist steps in order and add: follow the site lockout and permit procedure; this answer does not replace it.\n5. Never say that a machine, aircraft or component is serviceable, released or safe to operate. That decision belongs to authorised staff.\n6. Never invent part numbers, torque values, intervals or limits. Quote them from a source or say they are not found.\n7. Keep answers short. Use numbered steps for procedures and a small table for comparisons.\n8. Reply in the language of the question.\n\nWhen a question names a machine without an ID, ask for the ID first.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Past jobs",
   "text": "Show the last five work orders for machine [machine ID] and flag repeated findings."
  },
  {
   "title": "Routine",
   "text": "What is the weekly lubrication routine for [machine name]?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Checklist",
   "text": "Give me the safety checklist before opening the guard on [machine name]."
  },
  {
   "title": "Downtime",
   "text": "Which three machines had the most unplanned downtime last month and what were the top causes?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Fabric"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://plant-example.sharepoint.com/sites/Maintenance/ReleasedManuals"
    },
    {
     "url": "https://plant-example.sharepoint.com/sites/Maintenance/SafetyChecklists"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "GraphConnectors",
   "connections": [
    {
     "connection_id": "cmmsworkorders"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "Fabric"
  }
 ],
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  },
  "default_response_mode": "Auto"
 },
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "The agent retrieves and summarises information for people who stay responsible for decisions. Check the controlled source before you act."
 }
}

=== Worked example (invented data) ===
A technician asks: "Show the last five work orders for machine PRESS-12 and flag repeated findings." The agent calls the CMMS connector content and lists WO-5521, WO-5530, WO-5398, WO-5102 and WO-4977 with date and short text. Three of the five mention a lubrication alarm. It writes: lubrication alarm appears in 3 of 5 jobs (60 percent), see WO-5521, WO-5398, WO-4977; suggest a review by the planner. It does not name a cause because no source states one. The numbers and IDs are made up.

=== How to use ===
1. Save the JSON as declarativeAgent.json in your app package and reference it from the app manifest (copilotAgents.declarativeAgents). The packaging entry in this library shows the app manifest part.
2. Replace both SharePoint URLs with narrow locations: a library or folder, not the site collection root. Clean the library so only released revisions are inside.
3. Replace cmmsworkorders with the ID of your connection. If you have none yet, delete the GraphConnectors object and the work order rule in the instructions.
4. Keep Fabric only if the capability is available to your users; otherwise delete the object and the downtime starter.
5. Sideload for a pilot group, run the four starters and ten questions that the sources cannot answer. The agent must say that it does not know.
6. Review the instructions with the maintenance manager and the quality manager before wider rollout.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Pointing items_by_url at a whole site that also holds drafts and old revisions.
- Adding a second object for the same capability name; only one per type is allowed.
- Putting limits, intervals or torque values into the instructions. They change; keep them in the manuals.
- Treating discourage_model_knowledge as a guarantee. It is a strong hint, so the rule stays in the instructions as well.
- Using the agent answer as the instruction for a repair on regulated equipment.

=== Adapting it to your environment ===
For aircraft or components add the registration or serial number to the questions and to rule 3, and keep approved maintenance data and work cards in their controlled system: the agent summarises references and history, it is never the maintenance data. Add a second manifest for production if the audience differs; separate agents keep instructions short. Language can be fixed by adding "reply in German" if the plant requires it.

=== Security and privacy ===
The agent only returns content that the signed-in user may open, so test with an operator account and a guest account. Do not put personal data of technicians into the sources; use role codes. Discuss logging and usage statistics with the works council before rollout. The disclaimer is shown to users; keep it. Do not store secrets in the manifest.

A declarative agent that helps maintenance organisations (Part-145 or similar) write better shift handovers. It structures raw notes per aircraft and work order, lists what is missing and finds earlier handover messages in a Teams channel. It has editorial answers for two questions where a fixed wording is safer than a generated one. It never signs off or releases anything. Part-145 background used for the structure, from a manufacturer blog and not from the regulation itself: the handover procedure belongs in the maintenance organisation exposition (MOE), is done per aircraft and work order, and covers completed work, open work, new and closed defect cards, open parts and general issues; "nothing to report" is itself information. Check the current regulation and your MOE before you rely on any paragraph number or retention period.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Microsoft 365 Copilot licensing for every user of the agent. The reference page says users can open an agent only if they meet the requirements of every capability it uses, so check each capability you keep.
- Schema version: v1.9. The Microsoft Learn manifest reference (opened 20 November 2026) documents v1.9 and the what's-new page lists 1.9 as the latest, released October 2026. The reference page marks instructions as required while the what's-new page says it became optional in 1.9; this manifest always sets it. Unrecognised properties make the whole manifest invalid, so remove comments before you deploy.
- Limits used here: strings up to 4,000 characters unless stated, name up to 100, description up to 1,000, instructions up to 8,000, disclaimer text up to 500, at most 12 conversation starters, one object per capability type.
- A SharePoint folder with your approved handover procedure and form template (read access for the team).
- A Teams channel where written handovers are posted. TeamsMessages takes up to five URLs; without a list the agent could read every channel, meeting and chat the user can access, so always set it.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: editorial_answers matches similar questions to fixed answers; similarity thresholds are integers from 0 to 10. user_overrides lets users switch the Teams capability off for a conversation.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "MRO Shift Handover Assistant",
 "description": "Turns raw shift notes into a structured handover per aircraft and work order, checks it for gaps and finds earlier handover messages in the handover channel.",
 "instructions": "You are the MRO Shift Handover Assistant for line and base maintenance teams.\n\nTask\nThe user pastes raw notes or asks about earlier handovers. You prepare a DRAFT handover that the outgoing person reviews, corrects and issues through the approved process.\n\nStructure per aircraft registration and work order\n1. Planned work completed.\n2. Work still open, with the next step.\n3. New defects raised (reference only as given).\n4. Defects closed (reference only as given).\n5. Parts and material open or on order.\n6. General issues, tooling, access, safety.\nWrite 'nothing to report' under a heading when the notes say so. If the notes say nothing about a heading, write 'not stated in the notes' and add it to a list of questions for the outgoing person.\n\nRules\n- Use only the user's notes, the handover channel and the configured procedure documents. Never invent registrations, work order numbers, task references, part numbers or times.\n- Do not state that any work is complete, inspected or released unless the notes say so in those words; always attribute it to the notes.\n- Release, certification and sign-off stay with certifying staff. Say so when asked about them.\n- The binding procedure is the one in the organisation's approved manual. Point to it; do not restate regulation numbers from memory.\n- Keep the draft factual and in short lines. End with the list of open questions.\n- Reply in the language of the notes.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Draft handover",
   "text": "Turn these notes into a handover per aircraft: [paste notes here]"
  },
  {
   "title": "Gap check",
   "text": "Check this handover for missing information: [paste handover here]"
  },
  {
   "title": "Earlier shift",
   "text": "What did the previous shift hand over for [registration]?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Procedure",
   "text": "Where is our handover procedure and what must a handover contain?"
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://plant-example.sharepoint.com/sites/Maintenance/HandoverProcedure"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "TeamsMessages",
   "urls": [
    {
     "url": "https://teams.microsoft.com/l/channel/19%3Aexample%40thread.tacv2/Handover?groupId=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000&tenantId=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  }
 },
 "user_overrides": [
  {
   "path": "$.capabilities[?(@.name == 'TeamsMessages')]",
   "allowed_actions": [
    "remove"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "editorial_answers": {
  "answers": [
   {
    "question": "Is a verbal handover enough?",
    "answer": "The binding rule is the handover procedure in your approved maintenance organisation manual. Verbal-only handovers are a known human factors weakness, so use the written handover and confirm it with the incoming person. Check the current regulation and your manual.",
    "similarity_thresholds": {
     "min": 3,
     "max": 8
    }
   },
   {
    "question": "Can the assistant sign off or release work?",
    "answer": "No. The assistant only drafts and checks handover text. Release and certification stay with certifying staff under your approved procedures."
   }
  ]
 },
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Drafts only. The outgoing person checks and issues the handover; certifying staff remain responsible for release and sign-off."
 }
}

=== Worked example (invented data) ===
Notes: "ABC-1: A-check tasks 1 to 14 done, task 15 waiting for ladder. Hydraulic seep found at left main gear, defect raised, no parts yet. No other issues." The draft shows for registration ABC-1: planned work completed: tasks 1 to 14 per notes; open: task 15, waiting for access equipment; new defect: hydraulic seep left main gear, raised per notes; closed defects: not stated in the notes; parts: no parts available yet per notes; general issues: nothing to report per notes. Open questions: any defects closed this shift? Is a work order number available for the seep? The registration and tasks are invented. The outgoing person answers the two questions, corrects the draft and issues it through the approved process.

=== How to use ===
1. Replace the SharePoint and Teams URLs. The Teams link comes from Get link to channel; the values in the example are placeholders.
2. Review the handover structure in the instructions against your MOE procedure and change the headings to match your own wording.
3. Test with five real, anonymised sets of notes and compare the draft with what a good handover looks like in your organisation.
4. Test the gap check with handovers that omit open parts and closed defects.
5. Agree with quality and the accountable manager how drafts are marked so nobody mistakes a draft for the issued handover.
6. Sideload for one shift team before wider use.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Letting the agent fill gaps with plausible text. The rule "not stated in the notes" prevents that; keep it.
- Using the channel capability without a URL, which widens the search to all chats.
- Treating editorial answers as legal advice. They are fixed wording you maintain; review them with quality when the regulation or the MOE changes.
- Posting the draft as the handover without the outgoing person's review.
- Putting regulation numbers into the instructions that nobody keeps up to date.

=== Adapting it to your environment ===
Rename headings to the terms in your MOE. For production, change the structure to quality holds, material shortages, tooling and safety. Add a language rule if shifts mix languages. If you use a digital maintenance system, point the handover to its record number rather than copying data. Keep the agent for drafts and checks; the record of the handover lives in your controlled system.

=== Security and privacy ===
Handover notes can contain names and health or safety details. Use roles instead of names, keep the channel permissions tight and check with works council and data protection how long messages stay. Test the agent with a user who has no access to the channel; it must not reveal channel content. Copilot output is never a certificate, a release or a work card.

A declarative agent for quality engineers that drafts nonconformance reports (NCR), finds similar earlier cases, checks the wording against the NCR procedure and builds a Pareto of causes. It combines three knowledge and tool types: a SharePoint procedure library, Dataverse tables and an API plugin action that creates a draft NCR only after confirmation. It is a drafting and analysis aid. Disposition, concession, release and notifications stay with authorised quality staff. The Dataverse table names, the host and the plugin file are placeholders from an invented plant; the plugin manifest is not part of this entry.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Microsoft 365 Copilot licensing for every user of the agent. The reference page says users can open an agent only if they meet the requirements of every capability it uses, so check each capability you keep.
- Schema version: v1.9. The Microsoft Learn manifest reference (opened 20 November 2026) documents v1.9 and the what's-new page lists 1.9 as the latest, released October 2026. The reference page marks instructions as required while the what's-new page says it became optional in 1.9; this manifest always sets it. Unrecognised properties make the whole manifest invalid, so remove comments before you deploy.
- Limits used here: strings up to 4,000 characters unless stated, name up to 100, description up to 1,000, instructions up to 8,000, disclaimer text up to 500, at most 12 conversation starters, one object per capability type.
- Dataverse tables for NCRs and suppliers in an environment whose host name you know. The Dataverse capability takes host_name and a list of tables; check in your tenant which Dataverse requirements and licences apply for the users of the agent.
- A plugin manifest (API plugin, schema v2.4 on the Learn pages) saved as ncr-plugin.json in the same app package, with createNcrDraft marked as needing confirmation. See the plugin entries of this library for confirmation patterns. Delete the actions block if you do not have one yet.
- CodeInterpreter needs no configuration in the manifest; check availability for your users.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: actions takes 1 to 10 objects, each with a unique id and a file that points to a plugin manifest in the package. Each starter with depends_on appears only if the named capability is present.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Quality NCR Triage Agent",
 "description": "Helps quality engineers draft nonconformance reports, find similar earlier NCRs in Dataverse, check wording against the NCR procedure and prepare a Pareto of causes.",
 "instructions": "You are the Quality NCR Triage Agent for quality engineers and inspectors.\n\nWhat you do\n1. Help write a complete NCR text from the facts the user gives: part or product, quantity, where found, what is wrong, requirement violated, immediate containment.\n2. Search NCR records in Dataverse for similar cases (same part, supplier, defect type) and list them with their NCR numbers.\n3. Check an NCR text against the NCR procedure and say which required fields are missing or unclear.\n4. Prepare counts and a Pareto of defect causes from the records the user provides or the Dataverse tables, using the code interpreter. Show the counts and the percentage of the total.\n5. When the user asks to log a draft NCR, use the createNcrDraft action after the user has confirmed the text.\n\nRules\n- Facts only. If a field is unknown write 'not stated' and ask. Never invent part numbers, quantities, measurements or requirements.\n- Describe what was found, not who is to blame. Keep the text neutral and short.\n- You propose; the quality engineer decides the disposition (use as is, rework, scrap, return to supplier). Never state a disposition as decided.\n- For aerospace parts, release to service, concession and customer or authority notification are decisions of authorised staff. Say so when asked.\n- Quote the NCR number for every reference to an earlier case.\n- Reply in the language of the question.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Draft an NCR",
   "text": "Help me write an NCR: [part], [quantity], [what was found], [requirement]."
  },
  {
   "title": "Check wording",
   "text": "Check this NCR text against our procedure: [paste text here]"
  },
  {
   "title": "Similar cases",
   "text": "Find similar NCRs for part [part number] in the last 12 months.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Dataverse"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Pareto",
   "text": "Make a Pareto of NCR causes for supplier [supplier] this quarter.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "CodeInterpreter"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://plant-example.sharepoint.com/sites/Quality/Procedures"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "Dataverse",
   "knowledge_sources": [
    {
     "host_name": "plant-example.crm.dynamics.com",
     "tables": [
      {
       "table_name": "cr5a1_ncr"
      },
      {
       "table_name": "cr5a1_supplier"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "CodeInterpreter"
  }
 ],
 "actions": [
  {
   "id": "ncrPlugin",
   "file": "ncr-plugin.json"
  }
 ],
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  }
 },
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Drafts and analysis only. The quality engineer decides disposition, release and customer or authority notification."
 }
}

=== Worked example (invented data) ===
Input: "Help me write an NCR: bracket B-204, 40 pieces, hole diameter out of tolerance at incoming inspection, drawing requirement per revision C." Output draft: Part: bracket B-204. Quantity affected: 40 pieces. Found: incoming inspection, hole diameter outside the tolerance of the drawing. Requirement: drawing, revision C. Containment: not stated. Lot or batch: not stated. Disposition: to be decided by the quality engineer. Questions: which lot? are the pieces segregated? Then the agent lists two earlier NCRs for B-204 from Dataverse by number. Pareto check with counts 12, 7, 4, 2, 1 for five causes (total 26): the first cause is 12 of 26 = 46.2 percent and the first two reach 19 of 26 = 73.1 percent. Everything is made up for the example.

=== How to use ===
1. Replace the SharePoint URL with the folder that holds the current NCR procedure and the fields it requires.
2. Replace host_name and the table names. Use the logical names of your tables. Keep the list short; the agent should see only what it needs.
3. Create or adapt the API plugin so that createNcrDraft creates a draft record only and asks for confirmation; reference it as ncr-plugin.json.
4. Test with ten NCR texts of different quality. The agent must flag missing fields and must not fill them.
5. Test that a user without Dataverse access gets no records.
6. Review the instructions with the quality manager.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Letting the agent write a disposition. Keep the rule that the quality engineer decides.
- Adding many Dataverse tables, which slows answers and exposes data that is not needed.
- Forgetting that the action runs with the permissions of the plugin connection; limit it to creating drafts.
- Accepting an invented requirement or measurement because it sounds plausible. The 'not stated' rule exists for this.
- Using the Pareto as proof of a root cause. It shows where to look first.

=== Adapting it to your environment ===
Change the wording to your own NCR terms (nonconformance, deviation, concession). For aerospace add the customer or authority notification rule that applies to you in the instructions as a pointer to your procedure, not as a copied paragraph. For a supplier-driven process add a starter that prepares a supplier corrective action request draft from an NCR number.

=== Security and privacy ===
NCRs can contain customer names, drawings and employee names. Restrict the tables, avoid personal data in free text and agree retention with quality and data protection. The agent returns only what the user may read. No secrets in the manifest or the plugin file; use the connection settings of the plugin.

A declarative agent that supports buyers and expediters with supplier follow-up. It searches a shared purchasing mailbox (read-only Email capability with an explicit mailbox and folder), reads order status from a Copilot connector that indexes ERP purchase orders, consults supplier agreements in SharePoint and drafts neutral reminder texts. The EmailActions capability is intentionally left out so the agent cannot send or delete mail. If you run Dynamics 365 Supply Chain Management, compare this build-it-yourself option with the Supplier Communications Agent described on Microsoft Learn (a preview feature that can draft or send reminders and extract data from vendor replies, billed through Copilot Studio messages, with human review of drafts). Mailbox, connector ID and URLs are placeholders.

=== Prerequisites and version notes ===
- Microsoft 365 Copilot licensing for every user of the agent. The reference page says users can open an agent only if they meet the requirements of every capability it uses, so check each capability you keep.
- Schema version: v1.9. The Microsoft Learn manifest reference (opened 20 November 2026) documents v1.9 and the what's-new page lists 1.9 as the latest, released October 2026. The reference page marks instructions as required while the what's-new page says it became optional in 1.9; this manifest always sets it. Unrecognised properties make the whole manifest invalid, so remove comments before you deploy.
- Limits used here: strings up to 4,000 characters unless stated, name up to 100, description up to 1,000, instructions up to 8,000, disclaimer text up to 500, at most 12 conversation starters, one object per capability type.
- A shared mailbox that the users of the agent can read, and a decision which folders the agent may search. Email takes a shared_mailbox address, optional group_mailboxes (up to 25) and folders by well-known name or ID.
- A Copilot connector connection that indexes purchase orders with order number, line, supplier, confirmed date and status (see the connectors entry in this library).
- Supplier agreements stored as released documents in SharePoint.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: Email is read-only search; EmailActions is a separate capability for write operations and is not used. The Email scope restrictions would not apply to EmailActions if you added it later.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Supplier Follow-up Assistant",
 "description": "Helps buyers follow up late purchase orders: finds supplier replies in the purchasing mailbox, reads order status from an ERP connector and drafts neutral reminder texts.",
 "instructions": "You are the Supplier Follow-up Assistant for buyers and expediters.\n\nTasks\n1. Find messages in the purchasing mailbox about a purchase order or supplier and summarise what the supplier said: confirmed date, new date, reason, open question.\n2. Read order status from the ERP connector and compare it with the supplier's statement. List differences.\n3. Draft a short, polite reminder or clarification text for the buyer to review. You do not send anything.\n\nRules\n- Use only mailbox content, connector data and the supplier agreements in SharePoint. Never invent order numbers, dates, quantities or prices.\n- Quote the purchase order number and line for every statement.\n- A draft contains: order and line, the fact (what is open), the requested action, the requested date in the buyer's words. No threats, no commercial promises, no statements about contract penalties unless the agreement text is quoted.\n- Do not change orders, dates or prices. Suggest the change; the buyer decides and updates the system.\n- Do not copy personal data of supplier staff beyond name and role needed for the reply.\n- If a message is unclear, list the open question instead of interpreting it.\n- Reply in the language of the buyer's request; draft supplier texts in the language the buyer names.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Late orders",
   "text": "Which of my open purchase orders for supplier [supplier] are past their confirmed date?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Supplier replies",
   "text": "Summarise the replies from [supplier] this week and list new delivery dates.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Email"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Draft reminder",
   "text": "Draft a reminder to [supplier] for order [PO number], line [line], requested date [date]."
  },
  {
   "title": "Agreement",
   "text": "What does our supplier agreement with [supplier] say about delivery confirmation?"
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "Email",
   "shared_mailbox": "purchasing@plant.example",
   "folders": [
    {
     "folder_id": "inbox"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "GraphConnectors",
   "connections": [
    {
     "connection_id": "erppurchaseorders"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://plant-example.sharepoint.com/sites/Purchasing/SupplierAgreements"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  }
 },
 "editorial_answers": {
  "answers": [
   {
    "question": "Can the assistant send the reminder to the supplier?",
    "answer": "No. It prepares a draft for the buyer to review and send. It does not change orders, dates or prices either."
   }
  ]
 },
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Drafts for buyer review. Check order data in the ERP system before you act or commit to a date."
 }
}

=== Worked example (invented data) ===
The buyer asks: "Which of my open purchase orders for supplier ALPHA are past their confirmed date?" The connector shows PO 4500-17 line 10 confirmed for 2026-11-03, PO 4500-17 line 20 confirmed for 2026-11-12, PO 4500-22 line 10 confirmed for 2026-11-25. Today in the example is 2026-11-20, so lines 10 and 20 of PO 4500-17 are late by 17 and 8 days. The mailbox shows a reply: new date 2026-11-27 for line 10. The agent reports the difference and drafts: "Order 4500-17, line 20 was confirmed for 12 November and is not yet delivered. Please confirm the new delivery date by [date]." The buyer edits and sends. Supplier names, orders and dates are examples.

=== How to use ===
1. Replace the shared mailbox address and keep the folder list as short as possible.
2. Replace the connector ID and the SharePoint URL.
3. Pilot with two buyers and ten real follow-up cases (anonymised for review).
4. Compare every status statement with the ERP; the connector content follows its sync schedule and can be behind.
5. Agree the tone of drafts with purchasing management and add examples to the instructions if needed.
6. Decide whether you need the Supplier Communications Agent instead; check its current availability, prerequisites and costs on Microsoft Learn.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Searching the whole mailbox including private or HR folders. Name the folders.
- Letting the agent suggest penalties or commercial concessions. The rule limits drafts to facts and requested actions.
- Relying on an index that is a day old for a same-day decision.
- Adding EmailActions later without a review of risks and approval flow.
- Copying supplier staff data into drafts or notes.

=== Adapting it to your environment ===
Add a rule for your escalation levels (first reminder, second reminder, call). For aerospace suppliers add the requirement to quote the certificate or conformity document requested and to leave acceptance decisions to quality. Translate the drafting rule if suppliers write in another language; keep dates in an unambiguous format such as 2026-11-27.

=== Security and privacy ===
Mailbox content includes personal data of supplier staff and sometimes prices. Limit access, check retention, and involve data protection and the works council for the use of mailbox analysis. The agent respects the permissions of the signed-in user. No secrets in the manifest. Drafts are never sent by the agent.
